00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:59.460
So we've been glad,
too, this morning,

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:01.710
actually, to introduce
our Cloud IoT Edge.

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:03.810
There is actually another
session tomorrow morning

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:07.120
at 9:30 that drills
into IoT Edge.

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:09.210
This is really listening
to our customers, saying,

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:12.300
I love your AI, but I have
some constraints at the edge,

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:15.240
that I can't really use your
cloud platform to do that.

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:18.150
Sometimes my internet
connectivity is down.

00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:19.980
In the case of Aker
BP, sometimes it's

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:22.470
boats that are going
that can't be connected.

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:24.750
Sometimes it's because
the bandwidth constraint.

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:26.280
You heard about LG this morning.

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:29.219
They're taking pictures,
hundreds of pictures

00:14:29.219 --> 00:14:29.760
all the time.

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:31.176
They can't send
all those pictures

00:14:31.176 --> 00:14:33.810
to the cloud to do the
inference and come back.

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:36.120
They have to do it on-site.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:37.750
Sometimes it's compliance.

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:38.820
You have to get the data.

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:40.470
You have to leave
the data on-site

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:42.660
and you can't send it
outside of your premise.

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:45.580
And there's a lot of reasons
why Edge makes total sense,

00:14:45.580 --> 00:14:48.570
and we're really trying to
bridge between Cloud and Edge

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:49.630
together.

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:54.700
So Cloud IoT Edge is
actually two components.

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:57.300
One is our Edge ML
component, which

00:14:57.300 --> 00:15:00.240
is a runtime to run
TensorFlow Lite model.

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:01.860
And to get a
TensorFlow Lite model,

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:05.610
you take a TensorFlow model
that you train, maybe with an ML

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:07.920
Engine, or with Cloud
TPU, using the cloud,

00:15:07.920 --> 00:15:10.740
and we'll compile
that model for you

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:14.100
into a TensorFlow Lite model
and send it to the device.

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:15.030
How do we send it?

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:17.670
We have Edge IoT
Core, which manages

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:20.310
the security, the
connectivity with the cloud,

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:22.920
or the local processing,
local storage.

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:24.570
Anything you need,
you would expect

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:28.826
as an Edge device, that's
handled by Edge IoT Core.

00:15:28.826 --> 00:15:29.950
This is in alpha right now.

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:31.449
There's a [INAUDIBLE]
access program

00:15:31.449 --> 00:15:34.660
that you can sign up for
today to get access to this.

00:15:34.660 --> 00:15:37.710
This runtime can run on
Android Things, which is

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:40.500
a fully secure and managed OS.

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:43.050
So that's a full OS,
managed the same way

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:45.300
we manage an Android
device or phone,

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:47.700
except it's for Things,
so it's been optimized.

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:51.510
And we're also going to
support Linux OS as well.

00:15:51.510 --> 00:15:54.660
And the big announcement was
the hardware accelerator,

00:15:54.660 --> 00:15:57.690
so Edge ML will
support, obviously,

00:15:57.690 --> 00:16:04.720
standard CPU, general purpose
CPU, or GPU, or our Edge TPU.

00:16:04.720 --> 00:16:06.870
So Edge TPU was probably
the most exciting thing.

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:08.370
I don't know if you followed
the news a little bit.

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:10.230
There were so many
tweets, I was like, whoa,

00:16:10.230 --> 00:16:12.850
this is actually
pretty impressive.

00:16:12.850 --> 00:16:14.152
So people tend to like it.

00:16:14.152 --> 00:16:16.110
So you're seeing a little
bit of the size here.

00:16:16.110 --> 00:16:20.460
You can fit four or five of
them on a coin, very small.

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:23.850
Edge TPU has been really
worked in collaboration

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:28.140
with the AI research team, our
software team, and the hardware

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:34.170
team together to really bring
a very purpose-built hardware

00:16:34.170 --> 00:16:36.780
for inference of
machine learning models.

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:38.640
So it's really
that collaboration

00:16:38.640 --> 00:16:40.530
between AI research
and the hardware

00:16:40.530 --> 00:16:44.880
that make it possible, with a
very big focus on performance

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:46.920
per watt and
performance per dollar.

00:16:46.920 --> 00:16:48.820
So trying to make
it cost-effective

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:50.910
so it can be
everywhere, and trying

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:53.850
to make it very low power so it
doesn't consume a lot of power.

00:16:53.850 --> 00:16:55.500
You can put it on
smaller devices.

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:59.020
You don't have to have big
heat sink and things like that.

00:16:59.020 --> 00:17:02.010
So we really believe
that this Edge TPU

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:03.960
will transform just
dumb data collectors

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:06.810
into smart collectors
and smart devices,

00:17:06.810 --> 00:17:10.044
and open up a whole range
of new user applications.

00:17:10.044 --> 00:17:14.819
So this is really,
really exciting.

00:17:14.819 --> 00:17:18.500
So the goal of Cloud IoT
Edge, if I summarize,

00:17:18.500 --> 00:17:21.230
it's to enhance personal
reliability, making

00:17:21.230 --> 00:17:22.400
sure it can work any time.

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:24.740
You don't have to worry
about loss of connectivity.

00:17:24.740 --> 00:17:28.190
With IoT Core, you can
really deploy those models

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:32.150
to the cloud, and to the
edge, sorry, and apply them

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:35.480
where you need them most in
real time, very low latency.

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:37.700
We always focus on
security, obviously,

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:40.580
for the secure connectivity
with Edge IoT Core.

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:43.070
And keeping it
cost-effective at any scale,

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:47.150
because the more
cost-effective, the more

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:50.010
they're going to be out there.

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:55.100
We also thought about how do you
marry Edge and Cloud in a very

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:56.724
closed loop of learning?

00:17:56.724 --> 00:17:58.140
And that was very
important to us.

00:17:58.140 --> 00:17:59.720
We didn't just release
an Edge product.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:01.511
We wanted to make sure
that Edge product is

00:18:01.511 --> 00:18:04.680
an extension of your Cloud
in a closed loop learning.

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:10.121
So this is a visualization of
the data flowing into IoT Edge.

00:18:10.121 --> 00:18:12.620
You can do some learning, or
you can do some inference here,

00:18:12.620 --> 00:18:12.980
if you want.

00:18:12.980 --> 00:18:14.970
But you can send the
data out through IoT Core

00:18:14.970 --> 00:18:17.670
to the cloud, put it in a
database of your choice,

00:18:17.670 --> 00:18:19.310
or data repository
of your choice.

00:18:19.310 --> 00:18:22.910
Retrain your model with
Cloud TPUs, potentially.

00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:25.010
Then send that model
back to that device

00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:30.262
through Edge IoT, IoT Edge,
to have a local execution.

00:18:30.262 --> 00:18:32.720
So that closed loop is very
important because those devices

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:33.720
are not static anymore.

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:36.210
It's not just you send an
ML model and let it be.

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:37.770
You will improve it over time.

00:18:37.770 --> 00:18:38.690
Things can change.

00:18:38.690 --> 00:18:41.450
New problems can be
detected with images,

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:45.020
and you need to be able
to really effectively move

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:46.670
those models,
update those models,

00:18:46.670 --> 00:18:50.680
through this life cycle.

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:53.460
So we've seen LG this
morning in manufacturing.

00:18:53.460 --> 00:18:54.600
Very great use case.

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:58.110
This is a million-dollar
saving per line.

00:18:58.110 --> 00:19:01.050
That's pretty impressive, just
by putting that intelligence

00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:01.920
at the edge.

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:06.160
In retail, you can think about
hyperpersonalization of offers.

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:08.820
This is an augmented reality
view, where discounts could

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:13.080
be given to you based on who you
are and your purchasing habits.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:16.170
In automotive,
collision avoidance,

00:19:16.170 --> 00:19:19.330
detecting if somebody is
looking at their phone.

00:19:19.330 --> 00:19:21.210
That would be pretty neat.

00:19:21.210 --> 00:19:23.490
That would avoid a
lot of accidents.

00:19:23.490 --> 00:19:25.170
There's a lot of
traffic crowding.

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:27.240
There's a lot of
application for ML.

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:30.150
So those are just a few examples
where we've seen traction

00:19:30.150 --> 00:19:33.540
with some of our customers.

00:19:33.540 --> 00:19:35.390
So if I show you,
if we showed you,

00:19:35.390 --> 00:19:37.190
the little TPU, if I
give you that little

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:39.440
TPU today, you're going to
be a little bit in trouble.

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:40.856
You won't know
what to do with it.

00:19:40.856 --> 00:19:43.520
So we created a little
SOM that I have here.

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:45.080
That SOM is a System On Module.

00:19:45.080 --> 00:19:49.550
So it's a full module which
has a quad core CPU on it,

00:19:49.550 --> 00:19:54.710
has a Wi-Fi, has, obviously,
a root of trust and crypto

00:19:54.710 --> 00:19:56.000
from microchip.

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:59.345
And it has the Edge
TPU, obviously.

00:19:59.345 --> 00:20:00.710
Don't forget this one.

00:20:00.710 --> 00:20:03.709
So this is a SOM that's
production grade, so you can

00:20:03.709 --> 00:20:05.000
actually produce a lot of them.

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:06.416
This will be
available in October,

00:20:06.416 --> 00:20:09.559
so you can buy this in October
and start playing with it.

00:20:09.559 --> 00:20:11.600
The other thing that we
did, because, same thing,

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:13.760
if I give you this, you
won't be able to do much.

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:15.800
So we created that
development board.

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:17.480
This is our AIY team.

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:19.490
Created the development
board so I can just slap

00:20:19.490 --> 00:20:21.460
this thing on it, like this.

00:20:21.460 --> 00:20:22.522
And I clip it.

00:20:22.522 --> 00:20:23.480
I'm not going to do it.

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:26.120
Oh yeah, I did it.

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:27.140
That was good.

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:29.960
And then you have a full
system with gigabit ethernet

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:32.600
and USBCs, and all
the things, HDMI.

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:33.990
You can connect a camera to it.

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:35.090
You can TPIOs.

00:20:35.090 --> 00:20:37.640
You can really play with that
thing and really get going.

00:20:37.640 --> 00:20:42.230
This also will be available in
October for you to play with.

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:48.085
So we also work with partners
on the Edge TPU sites,

00:20:48.085 --> 00:20:49.710
so really working
with some [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:53.460
vendors and some of the
device and [INAUDIBLE] vendors

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:58.431
to leverage the Edge TPU into
solutions for our customers.

00:20:58.431 --> 00:21:00.930
So some of those partners here
are building either gateways,

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:04.510
or really leveraging the
Edge TPU for their use cases.

00:21:04.510 --> 00:21:08.100
So we try to really kickstart
that ecosystem of partners

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:10.890
focused on Edge TPU and
application of the Edge TPU

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:14.860
for our customers and for you.

00:21:14.860 --> 00:21:16.690
Let's talk about scalability.

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:18.527
Now that you're all
excited about Edge TPU,

00:21:18.527 --> 00:21:20.860
you're going to have a lot
of devices with intelligence.

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:22.901
You're going to need some
scalable infrastructure

00:21:22.901 --> 00:21:24.000
in the backend.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:28.020
So IoT Core was,
as I said earlier,

00:21:28.020 --> 00:21:30.710
was really meant to be
completely serverless.

00:21:30.710 --> 00:21:32.640
And you can start
with one device,

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:35.490
connect it, and
ramp up to millions

00:21:35.490 --> 00:21:39.590
of devices in production,
without really having to shard,

00:21:39.590 --> 00:21:42.920
having to plan for
memory CPU, anything.

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:44.670
Everything is done for
you and you're just

00:21:44.670 --> 00:21:46.260
going to pay as you go.

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:49.830
And this allows you to
go from POCs to pilot

00:21:49.830 --> 00:21:52.410
to a small deployment
to your production,

00:21:52.410 --> 00:21:53.940
really, with no effort.

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:56.680
And that's really
a big game changer,

00:21:56.680 --> 00:21:59.370
we think, in terms
of how it helps you

00:21:59.370 --> 00:22:01.230
in your digital
transformation journey.

00:22:04.010 --> 00:22:05.230
So we innovated in the Edge.

00:22:05.230 --> 00:22:08.200
Obviously, we also kept
innovating on the IoT Core

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:10.450
itself, and those are
some of the features.

00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:11.800
Some are better.

00:22:11.800 --> 00:22:13.330
Some are a little more early.

00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:16.240
For example, the Cloud
IoT provisioning service,

00:22:16.240 --> 00:22:18.160
that's an early access.

00:22:18.160 --> 00:22:23.260
So this one is about enabling
you to onboard and provision,

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:25.540
literally, millions of
devices we've had to have,

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:28.660
without having to go one
by one and provision them.

00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:33.610
So if you're buying a bunch
of crypto, for example,

00:22:33.610 --> 00:22:35.470
with microchip, you
buy all those cryptos,

00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:37.870
they're going to give you a
real ID and some information.

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:40.270
You're just going to put that
in the Cloud IoT provision

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:43.000
system and all those IDs will
be moved to your account,

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:45.280
so you don't have
to do that manually.

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:47.200
We did things like
gateway, which

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:51.190
is a way to pass through
the authentication of IP

00:22:51.190 --> 00:22:55.330
or non-IP devices, pass that
authentication to the cloud

00:22:55.330 --> 00:22:56.240
directly.

00:22:56.240 --> 00:22:58.930
So the gateway is not
the proxy for all.

00:22:58.930 --> 00:23:01.630
You really authenticate the
devices behind the gateway,

00:23:01.630 --> 00:23:04.300
even though they may
not be IP devices.

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:06.110
So that's an interesting one.

00:23:06.110 --> 00:23:06.940
We do groups.

00:23:06.940 --> 00:23:08.710
We do high-speed
messages to the device.

00:23:08.710 --> 00:23:11.001
If you need 100 messages per
second down to the device,

00:23:11.001 --> 00:23:12.020
we can do that now.

00:23:12.020 --> 00:23:14.290
And we added some extensive
logging capabilities

00:23:14.290 --> 00:23:15.905
to debug your system.

00:23:15.905 --> 00:23:16.780
So a lot of features.

00:23:16.780 --> 00:23:18.910
We're going to keep
going like that

00:23:18.910 --> 00:23:21.670
throughout the rest of
the year and next year.

00:23:21.670 --> 00:23:23.935
This is kind of the Core
system being improved.

00:23:26.371 --> 00:23:28.870
Now, I would like to welcome
another customer that is really

00:23:28.870 --> 00:23:30.640
changing the game in farming.

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:34.540
So Jon Friedman
from Freight Farms.

00:23:34.540 --> 00:23:37.030
You all know about Airbnb
disrupted hospitality.

00:23:37.030 --> 00:23:40.000
We kind of view Freight Farms
as disrupting the agriculture.

00:23:40.000 --> 00:23:42.580
So I'm happy to
welcome Freight Farms.

00:23:42.580 --> 00:23:45.010
I think before we start,
we're going to see a video.

00:23:45.010 --> 00:23:45.700
JON FRIEDMAN: That's right.

00:23:45.700 --> 00:23:48.075
ANTONY PASSEMARD: So I don't
know if I click this or not.

00:23:48.075 --> 00:23:49.330
No?

00:23:49.330 --> 00:23:50.815
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:24:09.625 --> 00:24:11.630
[END VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:24:11.630 --> 00:24:13.580
ANTONY PASSEMARD: Excellent.

00:24:13.580 --> 00:24:14.900
Well, thanks, and welcome, Jon.

00:24:14.900 --> 00:24:16.540
So tell us more
about Freight Farms.

00:24:16.540 --> 00:24:17.581
JON FRIEDMAN: Yeah, sure.

00:24:17.581 --> 00:24:18.290
Happy to.

00:24:18.290 --> 00:24:24.350
I did notice a few people at
their session description.

00:24:24.350 --> 00:24:26.450
I know you're dealing
a lot with containers

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:28.170
as it relates to the GCP.

00:24:28.170 --> 00:24:28.510
ANTONY PASSEMARD: That's true.

00:24:28.510 --> 00:24:29.210
That's true.

00:24:29.210 --> 00:24:31.830
JON FRIEDMAN: This is a little
bit different container,

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:33.955
So you're going to be
finding out about that today.

00:24:33.955 --> 00:24:36.650
But we use that as a pivotal
piece of our infrastructure.

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:39.770
We feel that shipping
containers and IoT are actually

00:24:39.770 --> 00:24:43.250
great building blocks for
the next level of food.

00:24:43.250 --> 00:24:45.350
We're looking to lower
the barrier of entry

00:24:45.350 --> 00:24:47.450
for anybody who's trying
to get into farming

00:24:47.450 --> 00:24:50.660
and make food supply
a lot more accessible.

00:24:50.660 --> 00:24:55.042
So getting into this, we
started looking at the data,

00:24:55.042 --> 00:24:56.750
but we were surprised
to find there's not

00:24:56.750 --> 00:24:58.950
a lot of data in agriculture.

00:24:58.950 --> 00:25:01.160
In one of the biggest
industries in the world,

00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:04.080
really hard to find consistent
data to build around.

00:25:04.080 --> 00:25:06.920
And the reason for that is--

00:25:06.920 --> 00:25:08.660
there's a few reasons
for that, actually.

00:25:08.660 --> 00:25:10.910
One, there's not a
lot of environments

00:25:10.910 --> 00:25:12.810
that are exactly the same.

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:14.570
So if you have a
farm in one place

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:16.820
of the world and
another farm halfway

00:25:16.820 --> 00:25:19.850
across the world, the
results they're getting

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:21.390
and the things
that they're doing,

00:25:21.390 --> 00:25:23.270
they're not going to match up.

00:25:23.270 --> 00:25:26.330
So all that data is very siloed.

00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:29.390
Another reason is as farms
have been consolidated

00:25:29.390 --> 00:25:32.450
over the years, that
generational knowledge hasn't

00:25:32.450 --> 00:25:33.860
been passed down.

00:25:33.860 --> 00:25:38.670
So we're in a very centralized
global food supply system,

00:25:38.670 --> 00:25:41.540
and the things that
have been leveraged are

00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:49.340
the crops, the chemical, and the
genetic attributes of the crops

00:25:49.340 --> 00:25:51.450
have been really focused on.

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:53.690
And the ones have been
prioritized are the ones that

00:25:53.690 --> 00:25:55.835
can withstand
things like drought,

00:25:55.835 --> 00:25:59.750
are resistant to pests, and
they can travel really far

00:25:59.750 --> 00:26:01.844
and hang out on the
shelves for a long time.

00:26:01.844 --> 00:26:03.260
So you notice, I
didn't really say

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:07.010
anything about taste
or variety or flavor,

00:26:07.010 --> 00:26:08.610
or anything like that.

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:10.640
So if you are a chef
and you're looking

00:26:10.640 --> 00:26:13.010
to get a unique
thing on your menu,

00:26:13.010 --> 00:26:14.630
and you want to
make a dish that's

00:26:14.630 --> 00:26:17.900
just very signature
to you, that's

00:26:17.900 --> 00:26:21.270
going to be a really hard task
with our current food system.

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:24.980
If you're in sourcing, or you're
in any type of food business

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:27.225
where you're trying to
bring innovation to it,

00:26:27.225 --> 00:26:28.850
it's going to be very
difficult for you

00:26:28.850 --> 00:26:30.710
to get something
that's only grown

00:26:30.710 --> 00:26:33.650
in a specific part of the world
shipped all the way to you

00:26:33.650 --> 00:26:36.210
in the quantity that you need.

00:26:36.210 --> 00:26:39.680
So it's very familiar to
minimum order quantities

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:43.440
you see in global manufacturing.

00:26:43.440 --> 00:26:47.360
So what we were
inspired by was actually

00:26:47.360 --> 00:26:49.130
3D printers, and
that technology,

00:26:49.130 --> 00:26:52.250
and how that shifted
a segment that really

00:26:52.250 --> 00:26:56.210
couldn't get into the game
of prototyping or small run

00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:57.200
manufacturing.

00:26:57.200 --> 00:26:59.630
And we put that in their
hands so a lot more innovation

00:26:59.630 --> 00:27:00.590
could happen.

00:27:00.590 --> 00:27:02.910
So that's where we
came in and said, hey,

00:27:02.910 --> 00:27:05.090
is there a way that we can
build a platform that's

00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:07.792
accessible to everyone, uses
some infrastructure that

00:27:07.792 --> 00:27:09.500
already exists in the
world that everyone

00:27:09.500 --> 00:27:11.660
has some logistic support for?

00:27:11.660 --> 00:27:13.260
That's the shipping container.

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:16.835
And IoT, which allows us to
get a lot of that transparency

00:27:16.835 --> 00:27:18.710
and traceability that
everyone's looking for.

00:27:18.710 --> 00:27:20.756
ANTONY PASSEMARD: So tell
us more about IoT then.

00:27:20.756 --> 00:27:21.890
How is that helping?

00:27:21.890 --> 00:27:24.710
JON FRIEDMAN: So
IoT is what creates

00:27:24.710 --> 00:27:26.400
those environments in the farm.

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:30.620
So it's internet of the
environment for us, right?

00:27:30.620 --> 00:27:33.230
When our customers are
looking at the farm,

00:27:33.230 --> 00:27:35.090
they're looking at
all the components

00:27:35.090 --> 00:27:39.360
that make up the perfect
scenario inside the unit.

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:44.750
So let's say you're
just getting started.

00:27:44.750 --> 00:27:46.430
IoT is going to be
what, basically,

00:27:46.430 --> 00:27:49.940
sets the environment around
a certain crop attribute

00:27:49.940 --> 00:27:52.920
and heightens that
across the way.

00:27:52.920 --> 00:27:54.170
So let me give you an example.

00:27:56.970 --> 00:27:59.630
If you are looking
into IoT as a way

00:27:59.630 --> 00:28:05.270
to orchestrate all the
different components,

00:28:05.270 --> 00:28:08.450
you can, in any environment,
create an environment that's

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:09.540
halfway across the world.

00:28:09.540 --> 00:28:14.690
So we have customers in
Puerto Rico, Dubai, Detroit,

00:28:14.690 --> 00:28:15.800
all over the place.

00:28:15.800 --> 00:28:18.730
And inside that farm, it's the
perfect day of summer every day

00:28:18.730 --> 00:28:19.230
of the year.

00:28:19.230 --> 00:28:21.740
It's a very unique
environment in there.

00:28:21.740 --> 00:28:23.420
We can also create
environments that

00:28:23.420 --> 00:28:25.970
are not of this world with IoT.

00:28:25.970 --> 00:28:31.390
So you could take the
nutrients composition of Italy

00:28:31.390 --> 00:28:35.270
and you can match that with the
air quality of Salinas Valley,

00:28:35.270 --> 00:28:38.570
pair that with the
CO2 density that

00:28:38.570 --> 00:28:41.660
would be next to
an active volcano,

00:28:41.660 --> 00:28:43.640
as well as giving the
plant the light spectrum

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:45.100
that it really wants.

00:28:45.100 --> 00:28:47.516
ANTONY PASSEMARD: I really
want to taste that lettuce now.

00:28:47.516 --> 00:28:48.930
JON FRIEDMAN: Don't you?

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:51.590
So yeah, so IoT is a
really central piece

00:28:51.590 --> 00:28:54.955
for us to build these
environmental recipes,

00:28:54.955 --> 00:28:56.580
to match what's out
there in the world,

00:28:56.580 --> 00:28:59.120
but also to create things
that aren't of this world.

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:01.430
ANTONY PASSEMARD: So
your customers, the chefs

00:29:01.430 --> 00:29:05.615
or the farmers, they use a
mobile app to do all that?

00:29:05.615 --> 00:29:06.740
JON FRIEDMAN: That's right.

00:29:06.740 --> 00:29:08.240
ANTONY PASSEMARD: Can you show
us, maybe, what you have here?

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:10.073
JON FRIEDMAN: Yeah,
I'll give you a little--

00:29:10.073 --> 00:29:13.675
ANTONY PASSEMARD: That's
probably the demo god a bit.

00:29:13.675 --> 00:29:14.675
JON FRIEDMAN: Let's see.

00:29:14.675 --> 00:29:16.680
It looks like it's casting.

00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:19.171
ANTONY PASSEMARD: Can
we cast on the screen?

00:29:19.171 --> 00:29:19.670
Oh, yeah.

00:29:19.670 --> 00:29:20.520
Wow.

00:29:20.520 --> 00:29:21.205
OK.

00:29:21.205 --> 00:29:22.330
So what are we seeing here?

00:29:22.330 --> 00:29:23.670
That's a real farm, right?

00:29:23.670 --> 00:29:24.795
JON FRIEDMAN: That's right.

00:29:24.795 --> 00:29:28.830
So I will keep it
upright for this demo.

00:29:28.830 --> 00:29:30.717
So this is what any
farmer who has bought

00:29:30.717 --> 00:29:33.300
a leafy green machine-- that's
what a Freight Farm is called--

00:29:33.300 --> 00:29:35.850
they would see this, no matter
where they are in the world.

00:29:35.850 --> 00:29:38.575
Inside the farm, they might
use it as their remote control.

00:29:38.575 --> 00:29:39.960
So if they want
to turn something

00:29:39.960 --> 00:29:41.376
on and off really
quickly, they're

00:29:41.376 --> 00:29:43.120
going to use this same app.

00:29:43.120 --> 00:29:44.620
So let's, for instance--

00:29:44.620 --> 00:29:47.850
this is actually a live farm
in Boston, Massachusetts,

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:48.684
right now.

00:29:48.684 --> 00:29:49.850
This is at our headquarters.

00:29:49.850 --> 00:29:53.370
So let's go ahead and we're
going to turn on the lights.

00:29:53.370 --> 00:29:56.340
So all the devices
are connected.

00:29:56.340 --> 00:29:59.110
We're just going to--

00:29:59.110 --> 00:29:59.700
beautiful.

00:29:59.700 --> 00:30:01.310
We turn that on.

00:30:01.310 --> 00:30:04.270
And now if we do
a quick refresh,

00:30:04.270 --> 00:30:08.380
we should see the lights start
to turn on in succession.

00:30:08.380 --> 00:30:11.290
You'll also notice that
the front lights are linked

00:30:11.290 --> 00:30:12.940
to the back right
lights and the front

00:30:12.940 --> 00:30:15.610
left lights and the
back left lights.

00:30:15.610 --> 00:30:18.700
So that ability to pair,
link, and create relationships

00:30:18.700 --> 00:30:21.370
within the farm is all
within their fingertips.

00:30:21.370 --> 00:30:25.030
We can also set programs
and timers around that.

00:30:25.030 --> 00:30:27.250
We can do [? column ?]
recipes, yeah,

00:30:27.250 --> 00:30:29.420
and I'll talk a little
bit more about that.

00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:31.630
Now, let's say I've
turned a few things on

00:30:31.630 --> 00:30:34.060
and I really like what
I've got growing there,

00:30:34.060 --> 00:30:38.770
but I want to track what that's
doing to the rest of the farm.

00:30:38.770 --> 00:30:42.790
So you can pull up your
historical analysis

00:30:42.790 --> 00:30:44.366
and see what effect--

00:30:44.366 --> 00:30:46.240
maybe one of the things
that you've turned on

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:49.180
has on another
thing in the farm.

00:30:49.180 --> 00:30:50.980
So that's great,
because you can really

00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:55.980
start to tune the balance of
things based on your needs,

00:30:55.980 --> 00:30:56.962
and so on.

00:30:56.962 --> 00:30:58.420
So you'll be able
to look at things

00:30:58.420 --> 00:31:02.650
like water temperature,
pH, the amount of nutrients

00:31:02.650 --> 00:31:07.560
that are in the water, CO2 in
the air, humidity, temperature.

00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:09.280
And there is a whole
range of devices

00:31:09.280 --> 00:31:15.460
that you can pair, create
dependencies around, put

00:31:15.460 --> 00:31:17.660
on timers, things like that.

00:31:17.660 --> 00:31:19.900
So let's say I'm really
happy with what I got here.

00:31:19.900 --> 00:31:22.750
I'm going to go over
to the Program section.

00:31:22.750 --> 00:31:26.440
These are all of the things
that you can map out,

00:31:26.440 --> 00:31:28.780
different dosing schedules,
different lighting timers,

00:31:28.780 --> 00:31:31.924
set different day
and night times.

00:31:31.924 --> 00:31:33.340
And then you can
save that recipe.

00:31:33.340 --> 00:31:35.467
You can save that recipe
as a unique thing for you,

00:31:35.467 --> 00:31:37.300
or maybe you want to
get another farm that's

00:31:37.300 --> 00:31:38.370
halfway around the world.

00:31:38.370 --> 00:31:39.911
Say you're a chain
restaurant and you

00:31:39.911 --> 00:31:43.150
want to have a unique
lettuce on your bun.

00:31:43.150 --> 00:31:44.980
That can be something
that you save, import

00:31:44.980 --> 00:31:46.660
to all of your farms.

00:31:46.660 --> 00:31:49.570
But you can also pull from
a community of recipes

00:31:49.570 --> 00:31:52.300
that other farmers
have created, or we

00:31:52.300 --> 00:31:58.020
have created for you, to improve
your performance of your farm.

00:31:58.020 --> 00:32:02.030
So actually, last year,
we developed a recipe

00:32:02.030 --> 00:32:04.850
to improve the efficiency of
all the models, all the way back

00:32:04.850 --> 00:32:08.420
to 2015, and updated
all those farms at once.

00:32:08.420 --> 00:32:12.710
So with this, and with Cloud
IoT, we see this as a way

00:32:12.710 --> 00:32:16.310
that we can continuously
improve these farms over time,

00:32:16.310 --> 00:32:19.220
use machine learning
tools to basically do

00:32:19.220 --> 00:32:22.684
this updating for us, and
always choose the best path.

00:32:22.684 --> 00:32:23.600
ANTONY PASSEMARD: Wow.

00:32:23.600 --> 00:32:24.542
Jon, this is awesome.

00:32:24.542 --> 00:32:26.000
Thank you very much
for your trust.

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:27.000
JON FRIEDMAN: Thank you.

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:27.702
Thanks.

00:32:27.702 --> 00:32:30.157
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:31.901 --> 00:32:33.900
ANTONY PASSEMARD: Let's
talk about security now.

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:35.560
Security is almost
my favorite projects.

00:32:35.560 --> 00:32:37.268
It's where I started
my career, actually.

00:32:37.268 --> 00:32:41.020
So I've always been keen on
having great security for us.

00:32:41.020 --> 00:32:43.650
And Google will take security,
obviously, very seriously.

00:32:43.650 --> 00:32:46.219
And trying to look at
it from the ground,

00:32:46.219 --> 00:32:47.760
that the Google
Cloud Platform itself

00:32:47.760 --> 00:32:49.900
is built from the ground
up with security in mind,

00:32:49.900 --> 00:32:52.660
and IoT Core is built
on top of that platform.

00:32:52.660 --> 00:32:55.620
So we really try to
think about security

00:32:55.620 --> 00:32:57.495
from an end to end standpoint.

00:32:57.495 --> 00:33:00.900
You're going from the devices,
the connectivity, the Edge,

00:33:00.900 --> 00:33:03.420
the platform itself,
and the application.

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:05.010
But today, I really
want to focus more

00:33:05.010 --> 00:33:07.320
on the device side, rather
than the cloud side.

00:33:07.320 --> 00:33:10.540
By now, you probably have seen
a lot of sessions around that.

00:33:10.540 --> 00:33:12.970
And one thing we've
done differently,

00:33:12.970 --> 00:33:15.330
when we launched
IoT Core, is the way

00:33:15.330 --> 00:33:17.880
you authenticate
devices to the cloud.

00:33:17.880 --> 00:33:19.620
We use what we call a JW token.

00:33:19.620 --> 00:33:20.940
We don't call it a standard.

00:33:20.940 --> 00:33:22.080
It's a JSON Web Token.

00:33:22.080 --> 00:33:25.860
It's pretty common to
authenticate against APIs.

00:33:25.860 --> 00:33:30.030
So we use a JW token to do the
authentication of the device

00:33:30.030 --> 00:33:30.840
to the cloud.

00:33:30.840 --> 00:33:33.820
The cloud is authenticated
through a center TLS session,

00:33:33.820 --> 00:33:35.730
but then the device
uses a JWT that it

00:33:35.730 --> 00:33:38.580
signs with a private key
and authenticates itself

00:33:38.580 --> 00:33:41.520
with a corresponding
public key in IoT Core.

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:43.470
It has the same
level of security

00:33:43.470 --> 00:33:47.160
as a TLS mutual authentication,
but it has a lot of benefits.

00:33:47.160 --> 00:33:49.710
It has no dependency
on the TLS stack.

00:33:49.710 --> 00:33:52.110
It's very small in footprint.

00:33:52.110 --> 00:33:53.910
You can easily
upgrade your TLS stock

00:33:53.910 --> 00:33:56.010
without disrupting
your application.

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:58.950
And it can also
authenticate non-IP devices,

00:33:58.950 --> 00:34:01.175
because non-IP devices
can create a JWT,

00:34:01.175 --> 00:34:04.470
pass it to our gateway, and send
it to Cloud for authentication.

00:34:04.470 --> 00:34:07.140
So a lot of really
good benefits.

00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:10.650
And actually, to demonstrate
the power of this,

00:34:10.650 --> 00:34:14.670
we have partnered with
Microchip to release actually

00:34:14.670 --> 00:34:16.679
what is fundamental.

00:34:16.679 --> 00:34:23.948
This is the first 8-bit MCU that
is securely cloud-connected.

00:34:23.948 --> 00:34:25.370
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:26.320 --> 00:34:27.060
Yes.

00:34:27.060 --> 00:34:30.260
This is the first 8-bit MCU
that is cloud-connected.

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:32.159
This is really a
revolution because 8-bit

00:34:32.159 --> 00:34:33.580
has been there for many years.

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:35.497
15, 20 years, people
have been buying 8-bit.

00:34:35.497 --> 00:34:36.580
People know what they are.

00:34:36.580 --> 00:34:37.540
They're cheap.

00:34:37.540 --> 00:34:39.219
They're really available.

00:34:39.219 --> 00:34:42.370
But until now, everybody was
saying, no, for JWT, 32-bit.

00:34:42.370 --> 00:34:42.969
Let's go big.

00:34:42.969 --> 00:34:43.694
Let's go big.

00:34:43.694 --> 00:34:45.110
Well, actually,
you don't need to.

00:34:45.110 --> 00:34:46.219
You can take this 8-bit.

00:34:46.219 --> 00:34:47.770
And because our
stack is so small,

00:34:47.770 --> 00:34:50.080
because you use a JW token
to authenticate devices,

00:34:50.080 --> 00:34:51.940
that entire JWT
authentication can

00:34:51.940 --> 00:34:54.239
be handled by crypto
that's on there,

00:34:54.239 --> 00:34:59.650
and the whole TLS is in
the Wi-Fi module itself.

00:34:59.650 --> 00:35:01.690
So because it's so
small, it fits in there.

00:35:01.690 --> 00:35:04.380
If you use mutual auth,
you cannot do this.

00:35:04.380 --> 00:35:10.660
This is only Google-specific.

00:35:10.660 --> 00:35:12.320
So this is really a cool thing.

00:35:12.320 --> 00:35:14.635
This is going to come
probably around October.

00:35:14.635 --> 00:35:17.440
It will be available around
October for you to purchase.

00:35:17.440 --> 00:35:21.790
But this will really put IoT
everywhere very securely,

00:35:21.790 --> 00:35:23.261
which is super important.

