and even operational
efficiencies and effectiveness.

00:37:15.100 --> 00:37:17.830
So it's really this
idea of transformation

00:37:17.830 --> 00:37:20.570
within the industry.

00:37:20.570 --> 00:37:22.990
And as we think about
that, this is really

00:37:22.990 --> 00:37:26.840
a catalyst for new business
opportunities, this IoT data.

00:37:26.840 --> 00:37:29.590
And not only that,
creating ecosystems

00:37:29.590 --> 00:37:32.870
that can leverage the data in
new ways, to drive new value.

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:34.810
So why you embark
on an IoT journey

00:37:34.810 --> 00:37:38.230
maybe for one particular
reason, but then that data

00:37:38.230 --> 00:37:41.160
starts to become invaluable in
a number of different business

00:37:41.160 --> 00:37:41.660
cases.

00:37:41.660 --> 00:37:44.590
And so this idea of
democratizing data

00:37:44.590 --> 00:37:47.830
is really, really key
to the future of IoT.

00:37:47.830 --> 00:37:50.420
Let's look at an example.

00:37:50.420 --> 00:37:52.030
So this is a customer of ours.

00:37:52.030 --> 00:37:55.900
So Midas out of Europe,
not too dissimilar to Midas

00:37:55.900 --> 00:38:00.130
here in North America,
provides automotive services

00:38:00.130 --> 00:38:03.890
to their customers, and
they do this across a number

00:38:03.890 --> 00:38:05.840
of European countries.

00:38:05.840 --> 00:38:08.300
But with the growth
of the connected car,

00:38:08.300 --> 00:38:11.360
they could see that their
market was being disrupted.

00:38:11.360 --> 00:38:13.460
And in their words,
they said, "We

00:38:13.460 --> 00:38:19.060
had an opportunity to be daring,
to disrupt ourselves and become

00:38:19.060 --> 00:38:21.590
part of this
connected ecosystem."

00:38:21.590 --> 00:38:23.970
And so Midas has
partnered up with Z,

00:38:23.970 --> 00:38:27.850
who provides an
OBDII dongle that

00:38:27.850 --> 00:38:31.600
attaches to the vehicle to
provide data from the vehicle.

00:38:31.600 --> 00:38:34.480
And then they partnered up
with a GCP, the Google Cloud

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:37.780
Partner, Tellmeplus, who
specializes in building

00:38:37.780 --> 00:38:39.880
machine learning models.

00:38:39.880 --> 00:38:41.950
And these machine learning
models that they built

00:38:41.950 --> 00:38:44.980
were all around
predicting failures.

00:38:44.980 --> 00:38:47.529
In this particular case, they
started with battery failures.

00:38:47.529 --> 00:38:49.570
You're in your vehicle,
you're going to start it,

00:38:49.570 --> 00:38:50.500
it doesn't start.

00:38:50.500 --> 00:38:52.880
Not a delightful experience.

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:54.820
So again, Midas
partnered up with Z

00:38:54.820 --> 00:38:58.060
who partnered up and created
this ecosystem with Tellmeplus

00:38:58.060 --> 00:38:59.860
to build a machine
learning model that

00:38:59.860 --> 00:39:01.401
could take this data
from the vehicle

00:39:01.401 --> 00:39:02.710
and predict battery failures.

00:39:02.710 --> 00:39:04.215
Of course, this
is only the start.

00:39:04.215 --> 00:39:06.340
There's many other things
that they're going to do,

00:39:06.340 --> 00:39:10.780
but what this enables them
to do was to become a driver

00:39:10.780 --> 00:39:13.840
companion to their customer.

00:39:13.840 --> 00:39:17.010
So now, they've built an app
that can provide information

00:39:17.010 --> 00:39:20.220
about the maintenance schedules,
that can provide information

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:22.740
about predictions on what
might be coming and happening.

00:39:22.740 --> 00:39:26.050
It can also provide
geolocation information,

00:39:26.050 --> 00:39:28.620
and many, many other
things in the future.

00:39:28.620 --> 00:39:30.702
They've become the
driver companion and part

00:39:30.702 --> 00:39:31.410
of the ecosystem.

00:39:31.410 --> 00:39:33.900
Of course, they're
looking to roll this out

00:39:33.900 --> 00:39:36.790
to about 100,000 customers,
but it doesn't stop there.

00:39:36.790 --> 00:39:41.730
This data, you can
imagine, can be leveraged,

00:39:41.730 --> 00:39:44.670
for them to drive down their
operational costs, managing

00:39:44.670 --> 00:39:47.700
their inventory, having
that spare part available

00:39:47.700 --> 00:39:49.050
before you even get there.

00:39:49.050 --> 00:39:51.750
I don't know how many of you
have taken your vehicle in only

00:39:51.750 --> 00:39:55.250
to find out they didn't
have the part you needed.

00:39:55.250 --> 00:39:58.370
It just happened to me recently.

00:39:58.370 --> 00:40:00.066
And then not only
that, but who else

00:40:00.066 --> 00:40:01.190
could value from this data?

00:40:01.190 --> 00:40:02.957
The OEM.

00:40:02.957 --> 00:40:04.040
The vehicle manufacturers.

00:40:04.040 --> 00:40:06.410
All this plethora of
data that they can now

00:40:06.410 --> 00:40:10.100
leverage to understand
their customer better.

00:40:10.100 --> 00:40:11.310
It's an ecosystem.

00:40:11.310 --> 00:40:14.870
It's an ecosystem built on data.

00:40:14.870 --> 00:40:18.225
I'd like to welcome John
Heard from Smart Park, CTO

00:40:18.225 --> 00:40:19.220
of Smart Parking.

00:40:19.220 --> 00:40:21.200
Maybe he can shed a
little bit more insight

00:40:21.200 --> 00:40:24.260
on another example, all
related to smart cities.

00:40:24.260 --> 00:40:25.674
So John, welcome.

00:40:25.674 --> 00:40:26.840
JOHN HEARD: Thank you, Jane.

00:40:26.840 --> 00:40:28.220
JENNIFER BENNETT: Maybe
give us a brief overview

00:40:28.220 --> 00:40:29.790
of what you do
with Smart Parking.

00:40:29.790 --> 00:40:31.040
JOHN HEARD: Yes.

00:40:31.040 --> 00:40:33.480
Thank you, everybody,
for being here.

00:40:33.480 --> 00:40:36.770
And let me just tell you a
little bit about Smart Parking.

00:40:36.770 --> 00:40:40.310
We're a company based out
of Melbourne, Australia,

00:40:40.310 --> 00:40:42.590
but we have offices
in New Zealand, where

00:40:42.590 --> 00:40:44.420
we do our development,
and we have

00:40:44.420 --> 00:40:46.790
sites in 17 countries
around the world,

00:40:46.790 --> 00:40:53.090
where we are about reinventing
the parking experience for you,

00:40:53.090 --> 00:40:56.060
for various customer
experiences.

00:40:56.060 --> 00:40:59.510
So they are also the
customer experience

00:40:59.510 --> 00:41:01.070
for the enforcement officer.

00:41:01.070 --> 00:41:05.520
I know you hate them, but making
their lives efficient and much,

00:41:05.520 --> 00:41:06.950
much more effective.

00:41:06.950 --> 00:41:10.070
Changing the experience
of finding a car park

00:41:10.070 --> 00:41:13.430
that's in a city, that you
know that a car park is just

00:41:13.430 --> 00:41:14.389
around the corner.

00:41:14.389 --> 00:41:16.430
You don't need to keep
driving around and around,

00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:21.290
looking for that car space
that may not be there.

00:41:21.290 --> 00:41:24.320
We also provide
payment and interaction

00:41:24.320 --> 00:41:27.050
for allowing you to do
payment for your parking

00:41:27.050 --> 00:41:29.310
right there from
your smartphone,

00:41:29.310 --> 00:41:32.880
or also connecting to your
existing infrastructure,

00:41:32.880 --> 00:41:36.170
the payment machines
on the streets.

00:41:36.170 --> 00:41:39.410
And by the way, we do
both on the road parking

00:41:39.410 --> 00:41:42.260
and also off street
parking, and multi stories.

00:41:42.260 --> 00:41:45.320
And so when you see
some car parking spaces,

00:41:45.320 --> 00:41:48.100
where they have the red
and green guidance lights,

00:41:48.100 --> 00:41:50.680
that's the sort of stuff
that we do around the world.

00:41:50.680 --> 00:41:51.160
JENNIFER BENNETT: Great.

00:41:51.160 --> 00:41:52.701
Maybe tell us a
little bit about some

00:41:52.701 --> 00:41:56.270
of the challenges that led
you to partner up with Google.

00:41:56.270 --> 00:41:57.890
JOHN HEARD: Good question.

00:41:57.890 --> 00:42:00.830
As we have been starting
to talk more and more

00:42:00.830 --> 00:42:04.010
with our customers, we're seeing
a change in the conversation

00:42:04.010 --> 00:42:05.750
that we're having
with our customers.

00:42:05.750 --> 00:42:08.000
No longer just about parking.

00:42:08.000 --> 00:42:12.060
It's actually about how do we
change the city experience?

00:42:12.060 --> 00:42:15.470
How do we make our lives
within their environments

00:42:15.470 --> 00:42:17.570
more effective and efficient?

00:42:17.570 --> 00:42:20.600
And parking, because it's
actually a well-understood--

00:42:20.600 --> 00:42:22.940
we know about paying
for our parking--

00:42:22.940 --> 00:42:26.990
it pays for that initial
establishment of the IoT

00:42:26.990 --> 00:42:29.270
infrastructure for a city.

00:42:29.270 --> 00:42:32.690
And so what we're seeing is that
the Smart Parking is actually

00:42:32.690 --> 00:42:38.510
deploying and delivering the
initial pillar, or tent pole,

00:42:38.510 --> 00:42:42.470
which enables a city to start
to broaden their intelligence

00:42:42.470 --> 00:42:44.390
services within the city.

00:42:44.390 --> 00:42:46.700
So it reaches out
into other things,

00:42:46.700 --> 00:42:50.480
such as public broadband,
and smart rubbish bins

00:42:50.480 --> 00:42:54.890
or garbage bins, smart
street lighting, and so on.

00:42:54.890 --> 00:42:56.630
It's essentially unlimited.

00:42:56.630 --> 00:43:01.370
But that required us to look
at the characteristics of what

00:43:01.370 --> 00:43:03.980
that type of information
now requires.

00:43:03.980 --> 00:43:07.880
We need to connect to anything,
because if that central gateway

00:43:07.880 --> 00:43:10.840
on the street lamp
post is doing parking,

00:43:10.840 --> 00:43:13.650
it's also now going to need
to connect to other things.

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:16.280
And so we needed a
new class of system

00:43:16.280 --> 00:43:19.640
that was not just about parking,
not just about just dealing

00:43:19.640 --> 00:43:21.410
with those transactions
for parking,

00:43:21.410 --> 00:43:24.410
but essentially
connecting to anything,

00:43:24.410 --> 00:43:26.930
communicating with anything,
and then processing

00:43:26.930 --> 00:43:28.600
that information in smart ways.

00:43:28.600 --> 00:43:32.111
So it's not just about
parking, but for smart cities.

00:43:32.111 --> 00:43:34.110
JENNIFER BENNETT: We've
talked a lot about Edge.

00:43:34.110 --> 00:43:35.870
Talk to us about Edge
and your thoughts

00:43:35.870 --> 00:43:37.542
on this new opportunities
at the edge.

00:43:37.542 --> 00:43:38.250
JOHN HEARD: Yeah.

00:43:38.250 --> 00:43:41.370
Edge is really critical
to our real world.

00:43:41.370 --> 00:43:45.810
As we know, sometimes when
you try to make a phone call,

00:43:45.810 --> 00:43:46.910
you can't connect.

00:43:46.910 --> 00:43:51.440
Sometimes when you have
really time-dependent things,

00:43:51.440 --> 00:43:54.230
the time it takes to
do a communication

00:43:54.230 --> 00:43:58.380
transaction to a backend
computer cloud system

00:43:58.380 --> 00:43:59.600
may be too long.

00:43:59.600 --> 00:44:04.610
An example is we process
many, many, many thousands

00:44:04.610 --> 00:44:07.250
of images per day,
and we recognize

00:44:07.250 --> 00:44:08.660
the number plates of cars.

00:44:08.660 --> 00:44:11.270
And that number plate is
used to raise a barrier arm,

00:44:11.270 --> 00:44:13.840
because you've preregistered,
for example, to go

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:15.380
into that car parking lot.

00:44:15.380 --> 00:44:17.060
Now, if that
barrier arm does not

00:44:17.060 --> 00:44:19.220
open within about
two seconds, you're

00:44:19.220 --> 00:44:23.630
starting to create a queue of
vehicles outside that parking

00:44:23.630 --> 00:44:26.070
space, and that really
becomes critical,

00:44:26.070 --> 00:44:28.550
especially at 10 to 9:00
in the morning when you

00:44:28.550 --> 00:44:29.940
need to get into the office.

00:44:29.940 --> 00:44:32.420
Well, more importantly, it
creates congestion, also.

00:44:32.420 --> 00:44:34.850
So the Edge computing--

00:44:34.850 --> 00:44:38.000
just one example of how the Edge
computing is really critical

00:44:38.000 --> 00:44:40.640
is to do that
processing in real time

00:44:40.640 --> 00:44:42.890
right there where you
need that information.

00:44:42.890 --> 00:44:46.370
And that Edge computing needs
to be quite intelligent as well,

00:44:46.370 --> 00:44:48.440
and that's actually what
we're so excited about,

00:44:48.440 --> 00:44:50.240
the HTPU capabilities,
because we

00:44:50.240 --> 00:44:53.120
can start to do a whole
variety of intelligence right

00:44:53.120 --> 00:44:55.939
at the Edge to guarantee
real time licensing.

00:44:55.939 --> 00:44:57.230
JENNIFER BENNETT: Yeah, I know.

00:44:57.230 --> 00:44:58.370
JOHN HEARD: That's
just one example.

00:44:58.370 --> 00:44:59.540
JENNIFER BENNETT: Fantastic.

00:44:59.540 --> 00:45:03.770
I was talking to a retailer,
and they always think about

00:45:03.770 --> 00:45:05.540
inside the four walls.

00:45:05.540 --> 00:45:07.820
And I said, one of my
biggest frustrations

00:45:07.820 --> 00:45:08.900
is finding a car spot.

00:45:08.900 --> 00:45:11.120
So when I talk about
ecosystems, it's like,

00:45:11.120 --> 00:45:13.940
how can we take, now, that
Smart Parking that navigates you

00:45:13.940 --> 00:45:17.240
through the journey that
you do in a day, where

00:45:17.240 --> 00:45:20.120
you're parking to go into a
building or going to the store.

00:45:20.120 --> 00:45:23.114
Right, so this ecosystem,
and I know that you probably

00:45:23.114 --> 00:45:24.030
face this quite a bit.

00:45:24.030 --> 00:45:25.515
It's really about
how do we start

00:45:25.515 --> 00:45:26.640
to enable these ecosystems?

00:45:26.640 --> 00:45:28.160
JOHN HEARD: Exactly.

00:45:28.160 --> 00:45:29.810
It was interesting,
John just commented

00:45:29.810 --> 00:45:31.900
about the internet of farm.

00:45:31.900 --> 00:45:33.710
And I was just thinking
as he was speaking,

00:45:33.710 --> 00:45:36.560
we're creating the internet
of parking for you.

00:45:36.560 --> 00:45:37.700
That's what we're doing.

00:45:37.700 --> 00:45:41.560
And actually, that's the mission
that I feel very passionate

00:45:41.560 --> 00:45:42.060
about.

00:45:42.060 --> 00:45:44.140
We're reinventing
that experience.

00:45:44.140 --> 00:45:46.340
And it's an experience that
we currently live with,

00:45:46.340 --> 00:45:48.000
and it sucks.

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:51.290
And so the opportunity
here of not just the

00:45:51.290 --> 00:45:53.540
parking and changing
that dynamic,

00:45:53.540 --> 00:45:57.680
but making it more cohesive
and more personalized to what

00:45:57.680 --> 00:45:58.494
I'm trying to do.

00:45:58.494 --> 00:45:59.660
It's not just about parking.

00:45:59.660 --> 00:46:03.171
Usually I'm parking there
for some other reason, right?

00:46:03.171 --> 00:46:04.670
I'm trying to get
to a store, or I'm

00:46:04.670 --> 00:46:07.400
trying to get to the office,
or trying to do something else.

00:46:07.400 --> 00:46:09.505
And it's an
inconvenience right now,

00:46:09.505 --> 00:46:12.210
and that's what our mission
is, to actually do that.

00:46:12.210 --> 00:46:13.970
JENNIFER BENNETT: You guys
have been a tremendous partner.

00:46:13.970 --> 00:46:15.410
We thank you for
your partnership,

00:46:15.410 --> 00:46:17.070
and thank you for
joining us today.

00:46:17.070 --> 00:46:18.070
JOHN HEARD: Thanks, Jen.

00:46:18.070 --> 00:46:20.100
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:22.915 --> 00:46:25.750
So as we think about
connected things,

00:46:25.750 --> 00:46:28.000
one of the things that's
really critical in IoT

00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:31.390
is this idea of
location intelligence.

00:46:31.390 --> 00:46:34.090
And Antony mentioned
Google, we've

00:46:34.090 --> 00:46:35.860
done a lot with
connected things.

00:46:35.860 --> 00:46:39.490
But it's no surprise, I'm sure,
that we have a lot of work

00:46:39.490 --> 00:46:41.770
into location intelligence.

00:46:41.770 --> 00:46:46.150
So if we take the very best
of maps and routes and places,

00:46:46.150 --> 00:46:47.740
and we start to
apply it to things

00:46:47.740 --> 00:46:51.340
like asset tracking, or perhaps
efficient routing for some

00:46:51.340 --> 00:46:54.460
of the most complex
fleet itineraries,

00:46:54.460 --> 00:46:57.160
tracking or visualizing
your connected asset--

00:46:57.160 --> 00:46:59.350
most importantly,
no matter where

00:46:59.350 --> 00:47:02.500
they are all around the world.

00:47:02.500 --> 00:47:07.330
This is geolocation, both
inside and outside, geocoding,

00:47:07.330 --> 00:47:11.290
distance matrix,
real-time traffic,

00:47:11.290 --> 00:47:14.350
real-time road conditions
all at your fingertips.

00:47:14.350 --> 00:47:18.010
Such a key asset to the
ecosystem of IoT, and we're

00:47:18.010 --> 00:47:19.990
very, very proud of
the work that we've

00:47:19.990 --> 00:47:22.220
done in cooperation with
our maps team as well.

00:47:22.220 --> 00:47:24.310
So no matter what
you're tracking,

00:47:24.310 --> 00:47:26.200
this solution, this
integrated solution,

00:47:26.200 --> 00:47:29.260
can really help you start
to manage your bottom line,

00:47:29.260 --> 00:47:32.150
grow your top line, and
manage the risk as well.

00:47:32.150 --> 00:47:33.820
Let's look at a few examples.

00:47:33.820 --> 00:47:37.810
So Vagabond is a company
that provides technology

00:47:37.810 --> 00:47:41.180
and operations for the food
and beverage service industry.

00:47:41.180 --> 00:47:42.550
So vending machines.

00:47:42.550 --> 00:47:45.264
And they embarked on this
journey of an IoT solution,

00:47:45.264 --> 00:47:47.680
where they connected vending
machines, getting information

00:47:47.680 --> 00:47:50.650
about stock, getting information
about the cash in the vending

00:47:50.650 --> 00:47:51.530
machine.

00:47:51.530 --> 00:47:53.238
But what they were
really looking forward

00:47:53.238 --> 00:47:56.350
to combine with that was this
idea of precise geolocation.

00:47:56.350 --> 00:48:00.010
And you can imagine these
vending machines may not always

00:48:00.010 --> 00:48:01.580
be in the easiest
places to locate.

00:48:01.580 --> 00:48:04.150
It could be in the
third floor dormitory

00:48:04.150 --> 00:48:05.770
or it could be in a
back alley, right?

00:48:05.770 --> 00:48:09.190
So this idea of having
world class geolocation

00:48:09.190 --> 00:48:10.600
was really, really critical.

00:48:10.600 --> 00:48:13.600
And now, they can overlay that
vending machine information

00:48:13.600 --> 00:48:16.900
on precise location
so that their drivers

00:48:16.900 --> 00:48:20.260
can be very efficiently routed
for restocking activity.

00:48:20.260 --> 00:48:23.080
And they've been able to drive
some tremendous improvements,

00:48:23.080 --> 00:48:25.720
in terms of both
the top line growth,

00:48:25.720 --> 00:48:28.300
as well as managing their
bottom line, and theft.

00:48:28.300 --> 00:48:31.540
So theft has been reduced
about 15% off the top line.

00:48:31.540 --> 00:48:33.010
So some really great example.

00:48:33.010 --> 00:48:35.710
Another example is a
company out of Australia

00:48:35.710 --> 00:48:37.060
called Fleetminder.

00:48:37.060 --> 00:48:39.940
And they provide vehicle
and asset tracking solutions

00:48:39.940 --> 00:48:41.680
in about 12 countries.

00:48:41.680 --> 00:48:43.330
And really, what
they wanted to do

00:48:43.330 --> 00:48:47.200
was be able to visualize the
location of these vehicles

00:48:47.200 --> 00:48:49.630
and combine it with some
of their GPS technology

00:48:49.630 --> 00:48:52.870
that they have, giving
dispatchers not only location

00:48:52.870 --> 00:48:55.660
information, but really
optimized routes.

00:48:55.660 --> 00:48:58.240
And so you can actually
see now even surrounding,

00:48:58.240 --> 00:49:00.250
the real-time
surrounding information,

00:49:00.250 --> 00:49:03.290
and really start to drive
a whole lot of use cases.

00:49:03.290 --> 00:49:06.220
For example, being able to
understand maintenance activity

00:49:06.220 --> 00:49:07.870
and where the closest
maintenance shop

00:49:07.870 --> 00:49:09.760
would be for that driver.

00:49:09.760 --> 00:49:12.700
Some really great
results from Fleetminder.

00:49:12.700 --> 00:49:15.760
About a 50% decrease
in overtime,

00:49:15.760 --> 00:49:18.010
which if anybody who's been
in the trucking industry,

00:49:18.010 --> 00:49:19.450
this is a huge problem.

00:49:19.450 --> 00:49:22.510
And a huge cause of turnover
is their time away from home.

00:49:22.510 --> 00:49:24.790
So some incredible
efficiencies that

00:49:24.790 --> 00:49:27.010
have been gained through
doing some of these things,

00:49:27.010 --> 00:49:30.340
not to mention really
reducing incorrect deliveries.

00:49:30.340 --> 00:49:32.830
So this has been really
game changing in terms

00:49:32.830 --> 00:49:35.270
